# UI visual consistency – PandaTrack

Interfaces must feel like one product: predictable controls, spacing, typography, and theme tokens. Avoid improvised one-off patterns when an established pattern already exists.

## Navigation that means "back" or "up" a level

When the user returns to a parent screen (listing, detail, or previous step), the control must match the **canonical back pattern** for that app subtree unless product explicitly requires a different treatment.

- **Canonical back control**: Use **`BackNavLink`** from `@/components/core/BackNavLink` for any link whose job is "go back" or "up" to a parent screen.
  - Use default **`appearance="pill"`** when the surrounding area already uses that pill chrome (for example store detail hero, edit page header).
  - Use **`appearance="button"`** in **form footers** next to a primary submit so cancel or "back to list" matches **`Button`** geometry and tokens (`outline`, `md`).
  - Do not use a plain text link or a raw `<a>` with ad hoc classes when **`BackNavLink`** already covers the case.
- **Other route trees**: Prefer **`BackNavLink`** when the pill back pattern fits the flow. Otherwise search the subtree for an existing back/up control (`_components/share/` or sibling pages) and reuse or extract a shared component rather than inventing a third style.

## Links that look like buttons

Use Next.js **`Link`** (with locale-prefixed `href` where the app uses locale routing) together with **`buttonVariants`** from `@/components/core/Button/buttonVariants` (and `cn()` when composing) so keyboard focus, hover, and theme tokens stay aligned with **`Button`**.

Do not give the same semantic action different visual treatments on nearby flows (for example back as text link on one page and as a filled secondary button on another).

## Spacing, type, and color

- Prefer **semantic Tailwind tokens** (`text-text-title`, `bg-background`, `border-border`, etc.) per `theme-light-dark.mdc`.
- Reuse **core** and **module** components for typography and controls before duplicating class stacks.
- Keep **responsive** behavior consistent with `responsive-design.mdc` (no accidental overlap; sensible tap targets).

## When this rule applies

Read and follow this rule when adding or changing:

- Page chrome: back links, breadcrumbs, or top-of-page navigation to a parent route.
- Footer actions on forms where a link navigates away (cancel / back to list) next to a primary submit.

For general hierarchy and flow, still follow **`role-ui-ux-design.mdc`**.
